\ safety.4 - traps as THROWs, and compile-only words (Iteration 556).
\ Loaded after extend.4 when the shell image is built, and never by the
\ kernel's build: that runs on the seed kernel, which lacks TRAP-ENTRY
\ and COMPILE-ONLY-XT - so this could not live in extend.4, which the
\ seed loads to cross-compile its successor.

\ ---- Traps, and compile-only words (Iteration 556) -------------------
\ Q21: a zero divisor or a bad address - the CPU's trap, which no CATCH
\ sees - comes here from the engine, on fresh stacks with the code on
\ top: -10 or -9, Forth 2012's. The message, then to the innermost
\ CATCH, as THROW - so `forth '1 0 /'` fails that command and the shell
\ goes on (A24). With no CATCH, or a HANDLER that cannot be a frame on
\ the return stack, exit 70, as the stack guards do: WARM would restart
\ Forth's read loop on a shell's input, and a corrupt handler would trap
\ again in THROW itself. Here, not in the kernel, for the S" strings.
: TRAPPED ( n --- )
  \ -28 is ^C (Iteration 571, A31): no message - the terminal echoed it,
  \ and the shell starts its next prompt on a fresh line
  \ -3 and -5: a stack's guard page (Iteration 586) - the return stack's
  \ guard also stops a data stack underflow past its top, and the engine
  \ cannot tell which, so -5's message names both
  DUP -28 = 0= IF
    DUP -10 = IF S" relf: division by zero" ELSE
    DUP -3 = IF S" relf: data stack overflow" ELSE
    DUP -5 = IF S" relf: return stack overflow or data stack underflow" ELSE
      S" relf: invalid memory address (segmentation fault)" THEN THEN THEN
    ETYPE 10 ECHAR
  THEN
  HANDLER @ DUP DICT-LIMIT U> SWAP R0 @ U< AND 0= IF 70 SYS-EXIT THEN
  (THROW) ;
\ COLD registers it in every process an image saved from here starts;
\ this process, now.
' TRAPPED START @ - TRAP-ENTRY !   ' TRAPPED TRAP-XT!
\ Q20: the words whose interpretation reaches the interpreter's own
\ return stack. COMPILE-ONLY adds more.
' >R COMPILE-ONLY-XT   ' R> COMPILE-ONLY-XT   ' R@ COMPILE-ONLY-XT
' I COMPILE-ONLY-XT    ' EXIT COMPILE-ONLY-XT


\ Including under CATCH (Iteration 591, the tenth review). The kernel's
\ INCLUDE-FILE keeps the input source and INCLUDE-POINTER on the return
\ stack and puts them back only when the file ends; INCLUDED closes its
\ file only then too. A bare Forth recovers anyway - an error goes to
\ QUIT, which resets INCLUDE-POINTER - but under CATCH, as in the shell,
\ a THROW out of an included file left INCLUDE-POINTER advanced by the
\ current line's length and the file open. About twenty failing
\ INCLUDEDs ran the lines past the 512-byte INCLUDE-BUFFER into the
\ image, and the shell died ("image uses an opcode this engine does not
\ have"). These are the kernel's, with a CATCH: whatever is thrown, the
\ source and the pointer are restored and the file closed, then it is
\ thrown on. The kernel keeps its own - its cross-compiler has no ['] -
\ and every later lookup finds these. The xts CATCH runs are kept as
\ offsets (!XT, @XT): ['] compiles an absolute address, wrong once the
\ saved image loads elsewhere - as DO-FORTH found at 571, and this at
\ its first try, when every include ended in a segmentation fault.
\
\ And the buffer's bound (Iteration 593, the eleventh review). The
\ kernel moves INCLUDE-POINTER on by #SRC - the current source's length
\ - so that a nested file's lines go after the line that included it,
\ with no bound: REFILL reads up to 256 bytes wherever the pointer is.
\ A file that includes itself, ten ordinary levels, or one include
\ from a forth command longer than 260 characters - #SRC is then the
\ command's length, not a line in this buffer - ran past INCLUDE-BUFFER
\ as before. And after an EVALUATE inside a file, #SRC is the string's
\ length, so a file included from it wrote over the including file's
\ live line. INCLUDE-LINE# is the innermost file's current line length,
\ 0 outside any file: the pointer moves on by that, and a file that
\ would not have 256 bytes left fails with "includes nested too deep".
\ The fid is still on the stack there, so INCLUDED closes it. The
\ review found the cause and wrote the fix; its limit: the enclosing
\ lines' lengths may add up to 260 bytes (256 on a 4-byte build).
VARIABLE INCLUDE-LINE#
: INCLUDE-LINES ( --- )
  BEGIN REFILL WHILE #SRC @ INCLUDE-LINE# ! INTERPRET REPEAT ;
VARIABLE INCLUDE-LINES-XT   ' INCLUDE-LINES INCLUDE-LINES-XT !XT
: INCLUDE-FILE ( fid --- )
  INCLUDE-POINTER @ INCLUDE-LINE# @ + 256 +
  INCLUDE-BUFFER 1 CELLS 508 + + U> ABORT" includes nested too deep"
  INCLUDE-POINTER @ >R INCLUDE-LINE# @ >R
  SID @ >R SRC @ >R #SRC @ >R >IN @ >R
  INCLUDE-LINE# @ INCLUDE-POINTER +! INCLUDE-POINTER @ SRC !
  0 INCLUDE-LINE# !  SID !
  INCLUDE-LINES-XT @XT CATCH
  R> >IN ! R> #SRC ! R> SRC ! R> SID ! R> INCLUDE-LINE# ! R> INCLUDE-POINTER !
  THROW ;
VARIABLE INCLUDE-FILE-XT   ' INCLUDE-FILE INCLUDE-FILE-XT !XT
: INCLUDED ( c-addr u --- )
  R/O OPEN-FILE ABORT" Can't open include file"
  DUP >R  INCLUDE-FILE-XT @XT CATCH  DUP IF NIP THEN   \ a THROW left the fid
  R> CLOSE-FILE DROP  THROW ;
